WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.580
How do you find a cause in nature without a lab?

2
00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:08.740
You often cannot randomly assign drought, competitors, or warming to wild

3
00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:13.080
populations. Yet nature imposes events of its own.

4
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.020
Following populations across years and sites turns those events into tests.

5
00:00:20.083 --> 00:00:24.883
In a natural experiment, nature supplies an event without random assignment.

6
00:00:26.223 --> 00:00:30.123
In a long-term study, you measure the same population every year.

7
00:00:31.523 --> 00:00:34.703
Returning matters: one year's weather can dominate a snapshot.

8
00:00:35.917 --> 00:00:39.737
Comparing sites in one year helps, yet space is not time.

9
00:00:40.997 --> 00:00:46.197
Young blue chromis, a reef fish, showed a crowding effect across reefs: a pattern in

10
00:00:46.197 --> 00:00:51.217
space. Showing numbers return to level needs records through time.

11
00:00:52.717 --> 00:00:57.537
Long records also catch time lags, as when winter food limits next spring's births.

12
00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:04.150
Without random assignment, you test causes by tracking years, comparing sites, and

13
00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:05.150
ruling out rivals.

14
00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:09.510
Mismatch is a gap between the prey peak and laying dates.

15
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:15.750
Both and colleagues compared Dutch pied flycatcher populations whose prey peaks

16
00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:16.750
differed in timing.

17
00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:21.930
If mismatch matters, decline should be steepest where the gap is largest.

18
00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:26.313
Suppose flycatchers decline where mismatch is large.

19
00:01:27.653 --> 00:01:32.113
Each year you track breeding success, survival of young, and wintering adult

20
00:01:32.113 --> 00:01:38.393
survival. If falling wintering adult survival explains most of the decline, is

21
00:01:38.393 --> 00:01:39.953
mismatch likely the main cause?

22
00:01:46.287 --> 00:01:51.927
Unlikely. Mismatch should show up mainly in breeding success and survival of young.

23
00:01:53.407 --> 00:01:57.667
Carry-over effects, where one season affects the next, can blur that line.

24
00:01:58.927 --> 00:02:04.027
So test habitat, predators and disease, and try feeding birds after the prey peak.

25
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.500
Still, wild comparisons can mislead.

26
00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:13.840
Two sites differ in soils, species pools, and history, confounders that mimic a

27
00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:20.120
cause. A statistical model exposes a rival driver it measured, not one it missed.

28
00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:25.480
Detection shows change beyond chance; attribution tests its cause.

29
00:02:26.583 --> 00:02:31.863
On Daphne Major in the Galapagos, Peter and Rosemary Grant tracked medium ground

30
00:02:31.863 --> 00:02:38.323
finches. 22 years after the large ground finch arrived, the two species together

31
00:02:38.323 --> 00:02:40.023
severely depleted the food.

32
00:02:41.303 --> 00:02:43.123
Their beak sizes then diverged.

33
00:02:44.223 --> 00:02:46.863
Yet a coinciding drought stayed hard to rule out.

34
00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:53.627
Comparisons also test migration load, a fitness drop from immigrant genes suited

35
00:02:53.627 --> 00:02:59.427
elsewhere. Timema walking-sticks live on two host plants, with colour morphs

36
00:02:59.427 --> 00:03:01.047
camouflaged differently on each.

37
00:03:02.347 --> 00:03:07.987
In 2007, Daniel Bolnick and Patrik Nosil compared 25 populations.

38
00:03:09.407 --> 00:03:13.807
Those receiving more immigrants were more poorly matched, and selection shifted

39
00:03:13.807 --> 00:03:14.667
their morphs more.

40
00:03:14.667 --> 00:03:18.067
It is a correlation, not a manipulation.

41
00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:25.720
In 2008, Rosenzweig and colleagues linked continental-scale warming to observed

42
00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:27.600
biological and physical shifts.

43
00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.740
Natural climate variation alone could not explain those changes.

44
00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:38.160
Still, data clustered in Europe and North America, a biased sample.

45
00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:43.840
Track years and sites, test rivals, and nature's records can reveal causes.
